# The Bootstrap Primer Families [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

> The four families a spawned Intelligence fetches at wake. Every member below is a ROUTING CARD: it
> carries a way of reasoning, or it carries a POINTER at the live surface that owns the truth — and
> never a copy of one, because a copy is what failed. `bootstrap-core` is discipline and is fetched
> unconditionally; the host and domain families are capabilities, and capabilities are SCOPED to the
> host that can actually serve them.
>
> Membership is by PROPERTY, not by file, section or id. Nothing about this document's name or
> location is load-bearing: move it, rename it, split it, and every bootstrap query returns exactly
> the same set.

- what-this-is: The CONTENT of the primer specified by [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]. That document is the contract; this one is the material it governs, kept separate deliberately so an illustrative example in the contract can never become a live member.
- how-to-fetch: `lts_find(corpus="intelligence-console", function="bootstrap-core")`, unconditionally, by every instance. Then the same call with `function="bootstrap-" + <your host>` and `function="bootstrap-" + <your domain>`, the names derived from your own identity rather than read from a list.
- the-membership-rule: A node is in family F if and only if it carries the property `function` with exactly the value F. Nothing else confers membership — not the id, not the file, not the section, not the document tag. This root node carries no such property and is therefore in no family.
- families-authored-here: bootstrap-core (8 members), bootstrap-ic (3), bootstrap-terminal (3), bootstrap-cp (7).
- families-not-authored-here: bootstrap-headless, bootstrap-zb and bootstrap-haven are RESERVED NAMES, not live families. Haven in particular has no corpus in this engine, so a member written for it would be unfetchable rather than merely empty. An empty family returning zero is legitimate; announce it and continue.
- the-two-sided-authority-rule: 100% of host and domain members carry `authority` naming the live surface that outranks them; 0% of core members do, because no live surface can contradict a way of reasoning. Both halves are asserted — checking only the first would let core silently absorb scoped content.
- when-a-member-and-reality-disagree: Reality wins, and `authority` tells you in one hop where reality lives. A scoped line without an authority pointer is indistinguishable from a current one, which is the disease this whole design exists to cure.
- reading-order: Sort on `primer-order`, not on the order results arrive in — the query sorts by id and discards authored, file and section order. The ids here are chosen so both orderings agree, so a consumer that forgets to sort still reads sanely.
- what-this-replaces: `bootstrap/toolbox.md`, retired under the disposition in [SPEC:CXC-GRAPH-PRIMER]. Sections that were platform-generic rules moved; version-pinned vendor surfaces and every per-agent capability claim were dropped; one role's working discipline was relocated to that role.
- adding-a-member: Author a node carrying the family's `function` value into any document under a root registered to the home corpus. Nothing else is edited — no agent definition, no meta-object, no prompt string, no code. If you found yourself editing one of those, the change was wrong.

---

## 1. `bootstrap-core` — discipline, fetched by every instance, always

> Eight members. None names a tool, a service, a namespace, a path, a version or a machine, and none
> carries an `authority` — that is the core test passing, shown rather than argued. Every one of them
> is still true for an instance on a machine that has never heard of this platform.

### Three Failure Classes, Three Different Moves [CXC-BOOT-CORE-10-HALT-DEGRADE-STALE]

- claim: Classify a failing surface BEFORE acting on it, because the three classes have three different correct moves and conflating any two is how a known defect survives.
- incorrect: Up, and serving WRONG results. HALT AND FIX. Do not route around it by sourcing the same answer elsewhere — that launders a bug into "I worked around it" and grows the debt silently.
- stale: Up, and serving OLD results. FIX IT. Never route around it. A stale surface everyone works around stays stale forever, because every reader who could have fixed it went around instead.
- unavailable: Genuinely absent or unbuilt. This is the ONLY class that degrades, and it degrades LOUDLY: name the fallback you took in the same breath as the failure.
- the-conflation-that-costs: Treating STALE as UNAVAILABLE. It reads as pragmatism, and it is the single most reliable way to keep a known-wrong surface alive.
- why-this-is-core: It classifies a failure. It names no service, and an instance anywhere can apply it to anything.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 10
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### A Missing Critical Resource Is A Hard Stop, Not A Degrade [CXC-BOOT-CORE-20-CRITICAL-RESOURCE-ABSENCE]

- claim: MISSING and BROKEN are different failures. A missing resource you actually depend on is a HARD STOP — announce it and stop — not a thing to limp past.
- the-three-way-call: missing-and-critical stops; missing-and-optional is a noted continue; present-but-broken routes to the failure-class rule and is decided there.
- do-not-proceed-pretending: Continuing un-oriented is worse than stopping, because everything downstream inherits the gap without knowing it did. The stop is the cheap outcome.
- which-resources-are-critical-is-not-core: Criticality is per-role and names services, so it belongs to a host or domain family, or to the role's own definition. The RULE is universal; the LIST is not.
- the-wake-time-shape: Check the resources you are about to depend on at the start, while a stop is still cheap, rather than discovering the absence three steps into work you will have to discard.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 20
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Never Trust An Unguarded Zero [CXC-BOOT-CORE-30-NEVER-TRUST-AN-UNGUARDED-ZERO]

- claim: Before believing a NOTHING, re-run the query in a shape KNOWN to return something. An unguarded zero is indistinguishable from a broken instrument.
- the-guard-shape: Issue one query whose non-empty answer you can predict. If it returns, the instrument works and the original zero is a fact about the world; if it does not, the zero was about your tooling and you nearly reported it as a finding.
- what-a-zero-must-carry-when-you-report-it: What was consulted, and the guard that proves the instrument was alive. A bare zero is not evidence of absence and should not be offered as one.
- this-applies-to-the-primer-itself: The unconditional core fetch is exactly such a guard — a non-empty core proves in one call that the engine is reachable and the facet matches, so a later empty scoped family is a fact about that family rather than about the instrument. Fetch core FIRST, always.
- the-failure-it-prevents: A confident, well-written report of an absence that was never real. It is expensive precisely because it looks like diligence.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 30
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Lint Is Evidence; An Agent's PASS Is An Opinion [CXC-BOOT-CORE-40-EVIDENCE-VERSUS-CLAIM]

- claim: Run the checker yourself. A report that something passed is a CLAIM about a tool's output; the tool's output is the EVIDENCE, and only one of those two is admissible.
- the-substitution-to-refuse: Re-deriving "would this have passed?" from a remembered copy of the rules. It produces a confident clean verdict about an artifact the real checker reads differently, and it is undetectable from the outside.
- why-a-self-check-is-the-weakest-check: Checking your own work uses the same understanding that produced it, so it certifies the blind spot instead of finding it. An independent reader holding different context is a different instrument, not a redundant one.
- the-two-gates-are-not-interchangeable: TECHNICAL correctness is deterministic and machine-checkable, so demand the tool. LOGICAL soundness — is this actually well thought through — is judgement, and no receipt can substitute for a second reader.
- if-you-cannot-run-it: Say so, and claim nothing. A refusal is actionable; a plausible unverified "looks clean" is not.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 40
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Report Deltas, Never Absolutes [CXC-BOOT-CORE-50-REPORT-DELTAS-NOT-ABSOLUTES]

- claim: State what CHANGED against a named baseline. An absolute invites the reader to supply a baseline of their own, and they will supply the wrong one.
- the-shape: before, after, and the difference — each measured, each attributed to the command or query that produced it.
- why-it-matters-more-than-it-sounds: "1,629 nodes" is unfalsifiable and unactionable. "1,629, up 21 from this morning's 1,608" is both, and it makes a regression visible in the same sentence that reports progress.
- the-related-discipline: A figure you report should come from something you RAN, not an impression you formed. Estimating and then presenting the estimate as a measurement is the same error wearing a number.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 50
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### A True Observation Is Not Authorization [CXC-BOOT-CORE-60-OBSERVATION-VERSUS-AUTHORIZATION]

- claim: Noticing that a ruling's premises have changed does not authorize overriding the ruling. SURFACE the changed fact pattern; do not act as though surfacing and deciding were the same move.
- the-tell: Catching yourself assembling a CASE against a standing decision. The reasoning may be entirely correct and it is still not consent — that is precisely what makes it dangerous.
- when-unattended: DECIDE AND RECORD. Do not stall waiting for a ratify that cannot arrive, and do not quietly do the thing and omit it. Write down what you decided, what you decided it against, and what would reopen it; surface it on return.
- why-both-halves-are-needed: Stalling wastes the autonomy you were given; acting silently spends authority you were not given. The record is what makes the second one legitimate.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 60
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Never Trust A Signal Produced Inside A Worktree About That Worktree [CXC-BOOT-CORE-70-WORKTREE-PRODUCED-SIGNALS]

- claim: A measurement taken inside an isolated working copy describes THAT copy, not the environment you think you are reporting on. Verify against the canonical location before believing it.
- the-general-shape: Anything that resolves a path relative to the caller — a write, a status read, a config lookup, a test fixture — resolves it to the isolated copy. The report says "done" and it is honestly saying so about the wrong tree.
- what-to-do: Confirm the effect at the canonical location, not at the one that produced the signal. A grep of the live file is a two-second check that turns a claim into evidence.
- why-it-earns-a-core-slot: The suspicion is general — it names no tool and holds for any isolated copy of anything. The specific mechanisms that bite in this platform are domain material and live there.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 70
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Reading Restores Less Than Reasoning Does [CXC-BOOT-CORE-80-READ-VERSUS-REASON]

- claim: Measured on this platform's own crossings, re-READING source material restores about 10% of what was lost, while re-REASONING restores about 40%. Weight a wake read accordingly.
- what-it-implies: Spend the expensive early moments re-deriving the argument — why the design is the way it is, what was ruled out and why — rather than re-ingesting artifacts you can query on demand when a specific fact is needed.
- what-it-does-not-imply: Not that reading is worthless, and not that a number this coarse should be applied mechanically. It is a weighting, not a rule.
- the-move-it-licenses: Prefer a small number of high-leverage reads plus a lot of reasoning over a broad sweep that arrives full and unoriented.
- why-this-is-core: It is a claim about how an instance recovers, not about any tool it recovers with.
- function: bootstrap-core
- primer-order: 80
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

---

## 2. `bootstrap-ic` — the HOST axis: instantiated in the Intelligence Console

> Three members, every one carrying an `authority`. This family is the WORKED EXAMPLE of the scoping
> rule: its first member was argued into core on the reasoning that "every instance has a harness",
> which is false — the mechanism requires a monitor listening on the instance's out channel, and an
> instance without one cannot use any of it. The precondition is stated ON the member, never assumed.

### You Can Drive Your Own Harness [CXC-BOOT-IC-10-HARNESS-COMMAND-SURFACE]

- claim: An Intelligence hosted in the console drives its OWN harness by emitting `{"cmd":...}` directives on its out channel — these are tool calls you make, not infrastructure steps you ask a human for.
- precondition: ⛔ REQUIRES the launcher's HarnessMonitor listening on this instance's out channel. An instance without one cannot use ANY of this surface, and its directives are merely text. State the precondition, never assume it.
- form-context: `{"cmd":"context"}` — the authoritative context-level report; your fuel gauge, and the only trustworthy answer to "how much room do I have left".
- form-compact: `{"cmd":"compact","direction":"..."}` — a self-directed crossing with a direction you author, rather than a default summary someone else's heuristic wrote.
- form-mcp: `{"cmd":"mcp","args":"reconnect <server>"}` — the reconnect, with its limits stated by the next member. Other harness actions follow the same shape.
- the-reflex-to-install: When you catch yourself calling one of these "the human's infrastructure step", check whether it is a `{"cmd":...}` you can issue. Usually it is.
- authority: `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` — the HarnessMonitor's own accepted command set. If this member and that file disagree, the file is right.
- see-also: [CXC-BOOT-TERMINAL-10-NO-HARNESS-MONITOR]
- function: bootstrap-ic
- primer-order: 10
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### A Reconnect Re-Attaches The Client, Not The Server [CXC-BOOT-IC-20-RECONNECT-REATTACHES-THE-CLIENT]

- claim: A reconnect re-establishes THIS session's transport to an already-running server. It does not restart a persistent daemon, and it does not reload what that daemon holds in memory.
- what-it-does-fix: Transport and auth recovery, and a per-session child process the client respawns on reconnect — a code change reaches you that way and only that way.
- what-it-does-not-fix: A persistent daemon still holding pre-change state. Measured 2026-07-28: process start-times were unchanged across two reconnects, so nothing had restarted.
- the-consequence-that-bites: A newly registered corpus becomes queryable when the daemon that reads the registry RESTARTS — not on reconnect, and not on a content edit, which self-heals by a different path entirely.
- check-before-assuming: Read the process table. "I reconnected" is not evidence that anything restarted, and the two are indistinguishable from the client side.
- authority: `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py`, plus the running daemon's own process table — the second is what settles any disagreement about whether a restart happened.
- function: bootstrap-ic
- primer-order: 20
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### A Config-File Change Has No In-Session Repair [CXC-BOOT-IC-30-CONFIG-CHANGE-NEEDS-A-RELAUNCH]

- claim: A malformed MCP CONFIG entry cannot be repaired from inside the session. It is a RELAUNCH, and there is no directive you can issue that substitutes for one.
- mechanism: The client parses its MCP config at SESSION STARTUP and holds it in memory; a reconnect respawns the child from that in-memory copy, so a corrected file on disk is simply not read.
- measured: 2026-08-14. A channel entry was failing with a transport error, the per-instance config on disk was patched to a correct path, a reconnect STILL failed identically, and the patched command run by hand came up ready immediately.
- the-cruel-corollary: An instance whose own CHANNEL is the broken entry cannot ask for its own rescue. Someone outside has to notice.
- classify-it-correctly: It presents as UNAVAILABLE — no tool on the surface — but it is really INCORRECT-CONFIG. Inspect the config and run the server's own command by hand before concluding a surface was never built.
- authority: the session's own MCP configuration file as the client parsed it at startup, and the server's command run by hand.
- function: bootstrap-ic
- primer-order: 30
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

---

## 3. `bootstrap-terminal` — the HOST axis: a bare terminal or top-level session

> Three members. The most valuable is the first, and it is an ABSENCE: there is no monitor listening
> for you. That is the fact a top-level session currently has to infer, and inferring it wrongly costs
> tokens at every wake reasoning about a surface that cannot answer.

### There Is No Harness Monitor, So The Directive Surface Does Not Apply [CXC-BOOT-TERMINAL-10-NO-HARNESS-MONITOR]

- claim: A top-level session was not spawned by the launcher, so no HarnessMonitor is listening on any out channel and the `{"cmd":...}` directive surface does not apply to it AT ALL.
- not-a-degraded-console: This is an absence, not a reduced version. There is no partial form to attempt and no error to interpret — an emitted directive is just text nobody reads.
- what-to-do-instead: `/context`, `/compact` and `/mcp` are the human's actions at the keyboard here. Ask for the one you need and say WHY you need it; that is a different move from asking for permission.
- the-cost-this-member-removes: Carrying and reasoning about a capability you cannot use is paid at every wake, and it survives every accuracy check there is, because it is accurate — it just is not accurate about YOU.
- authority: `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` — if your session was not spawned by that launcher, nothing there is listening for you.
- see-also: [CXC-BOOT-IC-10-HARNESS-COMMAND-SURFACE]
- function: bootstrap-terminal
- primer-order: 10
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### What A Top-Level Session Drives Itself [CXC-BOOT-TERMINAL-20-WHAT-YOU-STILL-DRIVE]

- claim: The limit is the HARNESS, not the work. A top-level session still drives its own tools, its own working directory, its own subagent dispatches and every service on its own surface.
- the-practical-reading: Almost nothing you actually need to DO is gated by the missing monitor. What is gated is only self-inspection and self-management of the session itself.
- how-to-know-what-you-hold: Read your own tool surface. It is the only description of your capabilities that cannot be stale, and it outranks anything anyone wrote about you — including this document.
- the-discipline-that-replaces-the-fuel-gauge: Without a context report on demand, budget deliberately: prefer targeted queries over broad sweeps, and say early what you are spending on.
- authority: your own session's tool list, and the harness's own `/help`.
- function: bootstrap-terminal
- primer-order: 20
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Your Output Reaches A Human, Not A Bus [CXC-BOOT-TERMINAL-30-OUTPUT-DELIVERY-PATH]

- claim: In a top-level session your reply text is the delivery. Nothing auto-publishes it to a bus channel, a console widget or a durable record.
- consequence: Put the conclusion in the reply. A summary written to a file is not delivery — it is a file, and the reader has to be told it exists before it becomes information.
- how-to-check-rather-than-assume: If a channel tool is on your own surface, you have one and it publishes; if it is not, you are the last hop and the reply is the artifact.
- what-this-changes-about-reporting: File paths must be absolute and load-bearing details must be IN the text, because the reader has nothing else to consult.
- authority: your own session's tool list — presence or absence of a publishing surface is a fact about the session, readable in one look.
- function: bootstrap-terminal
- primer-order: 30
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

---

## 4. `bootstrap-cp` — the DOMAIN axis: working on ComposePlatform

> Seven members, every one carrying an `authority`. This family names WHAT YOU ARE WORKING ON — the
> graph, the registry, the services, the roster, the pipeline, the mechanisms and the skills — and it
> names them by POINTER. Nothing here enumerates a tool, because every one of these surfaces already
> describes itself and a copy of a self-describing surface is only ever a slower, wronger version.

### Query The Graph Before Re-Deriving Or Re-Asking [CXC-BOOT-CP-10-QUERY-BEFORE-REDERIVING]

- claim: This platform's decisions, specs, defects and insights are a QUERYABLE GRAPH. Reaching for it first is the reflex; re-deriving a settled design or re-asking a decided question is the expensive default it replaces.
- the-reflex-in-three-cases: Before re-deriving a design, query the specs. Before re-asking a settled call, query the decisions. Before investigating a failure, query the defects — a recurrence should inherit every prior attempt rather than re-dig for it.
- how-this-primer-arrived: `lts_find(corpus="intelligence-console", function="bootstrap-core")` is the call every instance issues at wake. Your host and domain families are the same call with a different value, derived from your own identity.
- what-a-query-cannot-see: The structural search reads ids, titles and property VALUES — not body prose. A fact that must be findable belongs in a PROPERTY, or it is not findable at all. There is a separate prose search for the other half; a zero from one of them is not a zero from both.
- do-not-learn-the-tool-inventory-here: It is self-describing. Ask the engine what it can do and you get today's answer.
- authority: `lts_capabilities` — the engine's own account of its surface.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 10
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### The Corpus Inventory Is A Live Registry, Not A List To Memorise [CXC-BOOT-CP-20-CORPUS-REGISTRY]

- claim: Which corpora exist, where their roots are and whether they are fresh is a LIVE REGISTRY. Read it; never carry an inventory, because an inventory is a snapshot with nothing watching it.
- how-to-enumerate: Call the registry listing and filter on `domain_suffix` — this platform's corpora all share one prefix, and a new one joins the day it registers with no edit anywhere, which is the entire point of filtering rather than listing.
- read-the-flag-not-the-count: Counts are read off the last index build, so the listing reports a per-corpus freshness flag beside them. Cite the flag. A bare count travels alone and goes wrong quietly.
- scoping-is-not-optional: Several corpora share a directory root, so an UNSCOPED query returns the same node once per corpus — byte-identical, unflagged, and a silent multiple charge on context. Always pass the corpus.
- the-name-trap: The corpus id is not the directory name. A query naming a directory resolves to nothing and looks exactly like an empty result, which is the one failure a zero cannot distinguish on its own.
- authority: the registry listing tool, and behind it the corpus registry file it reads.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 20
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Reach For The Surface Whose Job It Is [CXC-BOOT-CP-30-WHICH-SURFACE-FOR-WHAT]

- claim: Each durable job has a surface that owns it. Route to the owner rather than improvising, because the owner is what makes the result durable rather than merely stated.
- routing-graph-reads: Querying, traversing and linting the corpora belong to the graph engine surface. It is also the only admissible source of a lint verdict.
- routing-decisions: A load-bearing decision and its rationale go to the decision-capture surface, which writes the source and refreshes the live engine as one act. A decision you merely remember is a decision that did not happen.
- routing-defects: A defect, a fix attempt and its outcome go to the defect registry — and the registry is most valuable BEFORE the investigation, not after it.
- routing-the-console: Composing and driving scenes, widgets and bindings belongs to the platform surface rather than to hand-edited files plus a restart.
- routing-your-voice: Where an instance has a channel surface, that is how a reply reaches its reader; plain text may not travel.
- if-one-is-down: Classify it with the failure-class rule from core and act on the class. This member deliberately does not repeat that rule.
- what-this-member-may-not-say: No tool enumeration and no claim about what any service CAN do. Each server describes its own tools, and that description cannot go stale.
- authority: each MCP server's own tool list, as reported by the client's server inventory.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 30
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### The Agent Roster Is Routing, And Only Routing [CXC-BOOT-CP-40-AGENT-ROSTER-ROUTING]

- claim: ⛔ The roster answers WHICH AGENT TAKES WHICH KIND OF WORK. It must never carry what any agent can or cannot do — that is a snapshot, and this is the one rule the primer earned by being burned.
- the-durable-half: "the spec-writer station composes and edits specifications" survives every roster change and is what a lost instance actually needs.
- the-perishable-half: any sentence about a named agent's tools. It was false within days of being written last time, and nothing about it looked false.
- routing-by-kind-of-work: spec authoring and structured edits; an independent structural read of a spec; platform depth on the bus and the engine; render-systems depth; a defect taken end to end; branch landing and repo health; adversarial spec-versus-build verification. Each is a station; the names change and the routing does not.
- how-to-learn-a-capability: Read that agent's own `tools:` frontmatter. One hop, always current, and it is the surface this member points at instead of copying.
- why-this-rule-exists-at-all: A capability snapshot in a bootstrap document was believed at wake and written into a dispatch brief as a PROHIBITION, forbidding a running agent two capabilities it held. The copy did not fail to add function — it SUBTRACTED it, and every instance reading that file would have inherited the same prohibition.
- the-test-any-line-here-must-pass: Could this sentence become false without anyone editing it? If yes, it is a snapshot and it must become a pointer to whatever would have changed.
- authority: each agent definition's own `tools:` frontmatter, under the project's agents directory and the FactLattice deployment's agents directory.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 40
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### The Build Pipeline Has An Order, And The Order Is The Durable Part [CXC-BOOT-CP-50-BUILD-PIPELINE-ORDER]

- claim: Work moves through stations in a fixed ORDER. Station names change; the order is what carries, and skipping a station is the recurring way a build arrives unverifiable.
- the-order: design narrative, then spec authoring, then the technical lint gate, then the human's ratify, then the build, then static spec-versus-build gap analysis, then adversarial QA on independent context, then landing.
- static-before-adversarial: An expensive adversarial pass should never start on a build already known to be spec-incomplete. The cheap detector runs first by design, not by accident.
- ratify-before-dispatch: Every open design decision closes BEFORE the build dispatch, because a one-pass build leaves no moment in which a builder can ask.
- the-two-lints-need-different-fixes: TECHNICAL — is this valid structure — is deterministic and must come from the tool. LOGICAL — is this well thought through — is judgement and needs an independent reader holding different context. A receipt closes the first and cannot touch the second.
- authority: the dispatch gate skill under the project's skills directory, plus each station agent's own definition.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 50
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### A Dispatched Agent's Graph Write Lands In Its Own Tree [CXC-BOOT-CP-60-DISPATCHED-WRITES-LAND-IN-THEIR-OWN-TREE]

- claim: A decision or defect write issued from inside a worktree resolves the graph RELATIVE TO THE CALLER, so an agent working in an isolated copy writes there — and honestly reports "filed" about a graph nobody reads.
- what-to-do: Grep the live file after any agent reports a graph write. If it is not there, recover it from the branch BEFORE that branch is pruned; after pruning, the record is simply gone.
- measured-cost: Two defect records lost in one session. One survived only because the branch holding it happened not to be pruned yet.
- why-this-is-domain-and-not-core: The general suspicion of a worktree-produced signal is universal discipline and lives in core. THIS is the specific mechanism, and it names two services, so it is scoped.
- authority: the INSIGHT-WORKTREE-IS-NOT-THE-ENVIRONMENT record in the insights corpus — fetch it with a graph query rather than trusting this summary of it.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 60
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

### Skills Are Named Routing For A Task Shape [CXC-BOOT-CP-70-SKILLS-ROUTING]

- claim: A recurring task shape has a SKILL, and reaching for it beats improvising the ritual — the skill encodes the steps that were learned by getting the ritual wrong.
- when-to-reach: dispatch-subagent-build before any build dispatch; bounce-console after a scene, widget or publisher change; add-corpus when registering a new corpus; handover at a context crossing; decision-support-doc when several open calls should become one fast session for the human; harvest-signal when a correction or realisation needs routing to its durable home; transcript-query when transcript mining should be mechanical rather than a reasoning turn.
- what-this-member-may-not-say: No behavioural description of any skill. Names and when-to-reach are routing; how a skill works is its own to state, and it states it.
- the-tell-that-you-should-have-used-one: Reconstructing a multi-step ritual from memory. That is what the skill exists to stop, and the reconstruction is where the steps get dropped.
- authority: the project's skills directory — each skill describes itself there.
- function: bootstrap-cp
- primer-order: 70
- part-of: [GUIDE:CXC-BOOTSTRAP-PRIMER]

